  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1107 PM CST SAT NOV 21 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery indicates a shortwave trough lifting\nnortheastward across Georgia this evening. At the surface, an\narea of low pressure is located over northeastern Florida, with a\ncold air damming (CAD) wedge along the East Coast. This wedge\nwill weaken as low pressure moves up the East Coast. While drier\nair has moved in aloft across Central Alabama, low-levels remain\nmoist. Weak low-level isentropic lift associated with low-level\neasterly flow will continue to result in areas of drizzle across\nEast Alabama, while an expanding low stratus deck will result in\nmild overnight lows. This stratus deck will be slow to erode on\nSunday due to a stagnant air mass with weak low-level winds and\nlimited mixing, but once clearing occurs temperatures will once\nagain be above normal. Meanwhile a cold front will be approaching\nfrom the northwest, but the shortwave trough will be weakening\nand out-running the front. Showers and a few thunderstorms will\ndevelop along the front over the Mid-South Sunday afternoon, but\nwill be weakening by the time they reach our NW counties Sunday\nevening due to a lack of moisture return/instability. Therefore,\nonly scattered shower activity is expected mainly in our NW\ncounties Sunday night.\n\nAs the front continues to move through on Monday, there will be a\nlow chance for showers as a weak southern stream shortwave moves\nthrough the area. The front will stall near the coast on Tuesday,\nwhile a strong CAD wedge will begin to build into the region as\nhigh pressure moves from the Great Lakes to New England and\nstrengthens to over 1040 mb. A stronger southern stream shortwave\ntrough moves in Tuesday night and Wednesday, eventually resulting\nin strong cyclogenesis along the East Coast. Increasing rain\nchances are expected Tuesday night, with light to moderate rain\nbecoming fairly widespread for holiday travel on Wednesday. Weak\nelevated instability may result in a few rumbles of thunder, but\nsurface-based instability is not expected due to a cool and\nstable air mass at the surface. Winds may also gust up to 30 mph\nat times. There is some ensemble spread regarding how quickly\nprecipitation exits as we go into Thanksgiving. Some clusters\nindicate some potential for light wraparound precipitation to\nlinger, depending on how far south a northern stream upper low\ngets pulled down. A synoptically active pattern remains in place\nFriday and Saturday. While the most likely scenario is for cool\nand dry conditions, some ensemble clusters do indicate a\nreasonable worst case scenario of low rain chances.\n\n32/JDavis",
